      usedToTreat: "Adjunctive treatment of obesity or overweight with qualifying comorbidity under product-specific BMI, age, and indication criteria. Medication supports a chronic disease plan; it does not replace nutrition, activity, sleep, mental-health, and secondary-cause assessment.",
      description: "Weight medications work through different bottlenecks. Phentermine is short-term labeled monotherapy that raises noradrenergic satiety/arousal. Orlistat blocks GI lipases so some dietary triglyceride is not absorbed. Naltrexone/bupropion strengthens hypothalamic POMC satiety and reduces reward feedback. Phentermine/topiramate ER combines catecholamine appetite suppression with topiramate neurocircuit effects for chronic therapy. GLP-1 or dual GIP/GLP-1 agonists increase satiety and slow gastric delivery. These are not interchangeable pills, and the correct question is sustained health benefit versus individual risk, not which causes the fastest first-week scale change.",
      mechanism: "Obesity reflects defended energy balance, not lack of willpower. Hypothalamic hunger/satiety, mesolimbic reward, gastric signaling, adipose biology, medications, sleep, and environment interact. Sympathomimetics increase norepinephrine; bupropion stimulates POMC neurons and naltrexone blocks beta-endorphin mu-opioid feedback; topiramate modifies GABA/glutamate/carbonic-anhydrase pathways; orlistat covalently inhibits gastric/pancreatic lipases in the lumen; incretin agonists activate gut-brain receptors. Product response checkpoints exist because continued exposure without meaningful early benefit adds cost and adverse effects without a favorable benefit-risk ratio. A checkpoint is not the same as an automatic toxicity date, and chronic therapy often requires long-term continuation because weight-regulating biology returns when effective treatment stops.",
      boxedWarning: "Warnings differ sharply: naltrexone/bupropion carries antidepressant suicidality boxed warning language; GLP-1/dual incretin products carry thyroid C-cell tumor warnings; phentermine/topiramate requires pregnancy prevention and REMS controls because topiramate exposure can cause oral clefts and fetal growth harm. Phentermine alone has no boxed warning but is labeled only for a few weeks and carries cardiovascular, tolerance, and misuse risks.",
      adverseEffects: ["Mechanism-specific GI effects, insomnia, BP/HR change, mood/cognitive effects, gallbladder disease, dehydration, nutrient deficiency, kidney stones, or hypoglycemia with diabetes therapy", "Weight regain after stopping an effective chronic agent because appetite and energy-balance signals return", "Pregnancy harm is a class-wide concern for intentional weight loss, with additional drug-specific teratogenicity"],
      contraindications: ["Pregnancy and drug-specific cardiovascular, seizure, opioid, glaucoma, hyperthyroid, MTC/MEN 2, malabsorption, or eating-disorder risks", "Do not combine products casually or duplicate ingredients such as bupropion, topiramate, phentermine, semaglutide, or tirzepatide.", "Assess secondary causes, interacting medicines, substance use, mental health, and realistic monitoring access."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Document percentage weight change, waist/comorbidity response, function, BP/pulse, nutrition, mood, adverse effects, adherence, and the exact labeled response checkpoint.", "Explain the why behind continuation or stopping: ineffective exposure should end or change, intolerable toxicity outweighs scale benefit, and effective chronic therapy may need maintenance rather than a brief cure model."],
      interactions: ["MAOIs prohibit sympathomimetic or bupropion-containing therapy within labeled intervals.", "Opioids cannot provide expected analgesia during naltrexone therapy and forced displacement can precipitate withdrawal.", "Delayed gastric emptying, fat malabsorption, CYP interactions, and carbonic-anhydrase inhibition alter other drugs in product-specific ways."],
      keyLabs: ["Weight/BMI/waist and percent response at the product's decision point", "Blood pressure, pulse, glucose/A1c, renal/hepatic function, pregnancy testing, bicarbonate, vitamins, or mood assessment as mechanism requires", "Cardiometabolic comorbidities, sleep apnea, medication contributors, and eating pattern"],
      nclexTraps: ["Phentermine monotherapy and phentermine/topiramate ER have different FDA duration frameworks.", "A response checkpoint is designed to avoid ineffective chronic exposure; it is not proof that everyone becomes toxic on that day.", "More weight loss does not excuse pregnancy exposure, uncontrolled hypertension, severe mood change, pancreatitis, malnutrition, or other red flags."],
      sourceNote: "Current U.S. obesity-drug labeling: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=40dd5602-53da-45ac-bb4b-15789aba40f9",
