      description: "Metformin is a biguanide that lowers glucose mainly by suppressing inappropriate liver glucose output between meals and overnight. It also improves insulin-mediated glucose use and changes gut-liver metabolism without directly forcing pancreatic beta cells to release insulin. That is why metformin alone rarely causes hypoglycemia and is usually weight-neutral or modestly weight-lowering. Its defining danger is not kidney damage caused by the drug; it is metformin accumulation during severe renal impairment or hypoxic/metabolic stress, which can contribute to life-threatening lactic acidosis.",
      mechanism: "Metformin reaches hepatocytes through organic cation transporters and alters mitochondrial energy and redox signaling. Proposed, partly overlapping mechanisms include inhibition of respiratory-chain complex I, changes in mitochondrial glycerophosphate handling, increased AMP relative to ATP, AMPK activation, reduced glucagon-cAMP signaling, and gut-mediated effects. The complete molecular mechanism remains unsettled, but the physiologic endpoint is clear: hepatic gluconeogenesis falls, insulin sensitivity improves, and intestinal glucose handling changes. Because metformin does not close beta-cell KATP channels or supply insulin, it does not independently drive glucose below the available fuel state. It is eliminated unchanged by the kidneys, so declining filtration and tubular secretion prolong exposure.",
