      adverseEffects: ["Diarrhea, nausea, abdominal discomfort, flatulence, and metallic taste, often reduced by slow titration and administration with meals", "Vitamin B12 malabsorption with possible macrocytosis, neuropathy, cognitive change, or glossitis during long-term therapy", "Rare metformin-associated lactic acidosis when accumulation and impaired lactate handling converge"],
      contraindications: ["Severe renal impairment with eGFR below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2", "Acute or chronic metabolic acidosis, including DKA", "Hold and reassess during severe hypoxia, shock, sepsis, major dehydration, acute kidney injury, or label-defined contrast/procedure situations"],
      nursingEssentials: ["Check eGFR before initiation and at least annually, more often when renal function may change. Metformin is not intrinsically nephrotoxic; renal decline matters because clearance falls.", "Trend vitamin B12 periodically and when anemia or neuropathy appears. Do not attribute new numbness automatically to diabetic neuropathy when a reversible B12 deficiency is possible."],
      interactions: ["Topiramate, acetazolamide, and other carbonic-anhydrase inhibitors can promote acidosis and increase lactic-acidosis risk.", "Iodinated contrast may precipitate acute kidney injury in susceptible patients; hold/restart rules depend on eGFR, hepatic/heart-failure risk, and intra-arterial versus venous exposure.", "Alcohol impairs hepatic lactate utilization and adds lactic-acidosis and hypoglycemia risk during poor intake."],
