      usedToTreat: "Adjunct to nutrition and activity for adults with type 2 diabetes who retain functioning pancreatic beta cells. Immediate-release and extended-release products have different timing instructions. It is not effective for type 1 diabetes and does not treat DKA.",
      description: "Glipizide is a second-generation sulfonylurea that makes pancreatic beta cells release insulin. It is shorter acting than glyburide and has inactive metabolites, so it is often the sulfonylurea chosen when renal impairment or older age makes prolonged hypoglycemia a concern, although it can still cause severe lows. Its benefit depends on surviving beta cells; it cannot manufacture insulin when beta-cell function is absent. The practical identity is therefore meal-linked glucose lowering with hypoglycemia and weight-gain risk, not a general insulin-sensitizing drug.",
      mechanism: "Glipizide binds the SUR1 regulatory subunit of the ATP-sensitive potassium channel on pancreatic beta cells. KATP-channel closure depolarizes the cell membrane, opens voltage-gated calcium channels, raises intracellular calcium, and triggers insulin-granule exocytosis. The pathway partially bypasses normal glucose sensing, so insulin release can continue when caloric intake is low; that explains fasting, exercise, alcohol-related, renal/hepatic, and dosing-error hypoglycemia. Functioning beta cells are required. The immediate-release product produces a prominent meal-related insulin response and is generally taken before meals, while extended-release matrix delivery spreads exposure across the day and must be swallowed intact.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("All sulfonylureas can cause severe hypoglycemia, including seizure, coma, and death. Risk rises with missed meals, malnutrition, prolonged exercise, alcohol, renal or hepatic dysfunction, older age, and combination glucose-lowering therapy. Historical class labeling also discusses increased cardiovascular mortality observed with an older sulfonylurea; use individualized contemporary cardiovascular-risk therapy rather than assuming every agent has equivalent outcome evidence."),
      adverseEffects: ["Hypoglycemia, sometimes prolonged or recurrent", "Weight gain, nausea, constipation/diarrhea, dizziness, headache, and photosensitivity", "Rare cholestatic/hepatocellular injury, cytopenias, hyponatremia, severe skin reaction, or hemolysis in G6PD deficiency"],
