      description: "Glyburide, also called glibenclamide, is a potent second-generation sulfonylurea that drives endogenous insulin release. Its longer action and active metabolites make hypoglycemia more persistent than with glipizide, especially when renal clearance is reduced, meals are missed, or the patient is older, frail, or malnourished. A first carbohydrate treatment may raise glucose temporarily while drug-driven insulin secretion continues, so recurrence is a defining danger. This is why glyburide is generally avoided in older adults and used cautiously or replaced in kidney impairment.",
      mechanism: "Glyburide binds beta-cell SUR1 and closes the Kir6.2-containing ATP-sensitive potassium channel. Membrane depolarization opens voltage-gated calcium channels; calcium influx triggers insulin-vesicle fusion and secretion. Because the drug pharmacologically bypasses glucose-dependent KATP gating, insulin release can outlast a meal and continue as glucose falls. Hepatic metabolism produces metabolites with glucose-lowering activity that rely partly on renal elimination, explaining prolonged or recurrent hypoglycemia in kidney dysfunction. The drug requires functioning beta cells and does not correct absent insulin or ketogenesis in type 1 diabetes.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("All sulfonylureas can produce severe hypoglycemia with seizure, coma, neurologic injury, and death. Glyburide's duration and active metabolites make recurrence especially important in older, malnourished, renally impaired, or hepatically impaired patients. Hospital observation and repeated glucose assessment may be needed after severe exposure; dextrose alone can provoke more insulin release in overdose, so toxicology-directed octreotide may be required."),
      adverseEffects: ["Severe, prolonged, or recurrent hypoglycemia", "Weight gain, nausea, fullness, heartburn, rash, pruritus, and photosensitivity", "Rare cholestatic jaundice/hepatitis, cytopenias, hyponatremia, severe skin reaction, and G6PD-related hemolysis"],
