      aliases: ["Actos", "pioglitazone hydrochloride", "pioglitizone", "thiazolidinedione", "TZD diabetes drug", "PPAR gamma agonist"],
      brandExamples: ["Actos"],
      class: "Thiazolidinedione insulin sensitizer; nuclear PPAR-gamma receptor agonist",
      classPathway: ["Endocrine medication", "Diabetes medication", "Thiazolidinedione", "PPAR-gamma transcriptional insulin sensitizer"],
      usedToTreat: "Adjunct to nutrition and activity for adults with type 2 diabetes. It improves insulin sensitivity only when endogenous or administered insulin is present. It does not treat type 1 diabetes or DKA and is not a rapid correction medicine.",
      description: "Pioglitazone activates the nuclear PPAR-gamma receptor to reprogram gene transcription in adipose tissue, skeletal muscle, and liver, gradually improving insulin sensitivity; it is a thiazolidinedione insulin sensitizer. Benefit develops over weeks because proteins and tissue metabolism must remodel; a single dose does not rapidly lower a high glucose. The same biology promotes adipocyte lipid storage and renal sodium retention, so weight gain and edema can occur and may precipitate or worsen heart failure. A falling A1c therefore does not make new dyspnea, orthopnea, or rapid fluid weight acceptable.",
      mechanism: "Pioglitazone binds the nuclear receptor PPAR-gamma, which heterodimerizes with retinoid X receptor and binds peroxisome-proliferator response elements to alter transcription. It increases adiponectin, improves insulin-receptor signaling, shifts free fatty acids away from liver and skeletal muscle toward subcutaneous adipose storage, and reduces hepatic glucose output. Gene transcription explains the delayed onset. PPAR-gamma activity in the distal nephron promotes sodium reabsorption and plasma-volume expansion; insulin coadministration amplifies edema. Bone remodeling can shift toward adipogenesis and away from osteoblast formation, helping explain fracture risk, especially in women.",
      boxedWarning: "BOXED WARNING: Thiazolidinediones, including pioglitazone, can cause or exacerbate congestive heart failure. After initiation and dose increases, monitor rapid weight gain, edema, dyspnea, orthopnea, and fatigue. If heart failure develops, treat it and consider dose reduction or discontinuation. Pioglitazone is not recommended in symptomatic heart failure, and initiation in established NYHA class III or IV heart failure is contraindicated.",
      adverseEffects: ["Edema, plasma-volume expansion, weight gain, and heart-failure exacerbation", "Fractures, especially distal limb fractures in women; macular edema and visual change", "Possible bladder-cancer risk signal, ovulation in anovulatory premenopausal patients, anemia/hemodilution, and rare liver injury"],
      contraindications: ["Initiation in NYHA class III or IV heart failure; avoid/reassess symptomatic heart failure", "Active bladder cancer and caution with prior bladder cancer or unexplained hematuria", "Type 1 diabetes or DKA; evaluate active liver disease before use"],
      nursingEssentials: ["Record baseline weight, edema, respiratory status, and heart-failure history; trend rapid changes after initiation or titration because fluid can accumulate before A1c benefit is evident.", "Teach patients who can become pregnant that ovulation may resume when insulin resistance improves, so contraception needs can change."],
      interactions: ["Insulin increases edema and heart-failure risk.", "Gemfibrozil inhibits CYP2C8 and substantially raises pioglitazone exposure; rifampin lowers it.", "Pioglitazone may reduce hormonal-contraceptive exposure at higher doses under some labeling; review product-specific counseling."],
      keyLabs: ["A1c/glucose response over weeks", "Weight, edema, dyspnea/orthopnea, and heart-failure status", "Liver tests before treatment; urinalysis/urologic evaluation for hematuria and bone/eye assessment when indicated"],
      nclexTraps: ["Pioglitazone lowers insulin resistance; it does not force immediate insulin release.", "Weight gain can represent dangerous fluid retention rather than improved nutrition.", "No routine periodic liver panel is required in an asymptomatic patient with normal baseline under current labeling, but liver symptoms require prompt testing."],
      populationRisks: populationRisks("Pediatric safety/effectiveness is not established; bone and weight effects are important concerns.", "Heart-failure, fracture, macular-edema, and bladder-risk assessment often makes another class preferable.", "Pioglitazone can restore ovulation; pregnancy should be recognized promptly and therapy reassessed. Animal milk transfer and limited human lactation data require an individualized plan."),
