      description: "Sitagliptin is an oral DPP-4 inhibitor that protects the body's own GLP-1 and GIP incretin hormones from rapid enzymatic breakdown. Active incretins then amplify insulin secretion when glucose is elevated and suppress inappropriate glucagon, so monotherapy has low hypoglycemia risk and is generally weight-neutral. It is less potent for weight loss and gastric slowing than an injected or oral GLP-1 receptor agonist because it extends physiologic hormone levels rather than directly driving the receptor at pharmacologic exposure. Renal elimination makes eGFR-based dose selection central.",
      mechanism: "DPP-4 is a serine protease that cleaves active GLP-1 and GIP within minutes. Sitagliptin selectively inhibits this enzyme, increasing intact incretin concentrations. GLP-1/GIP activate Gs-coupled receptors on pancreatic beta cells, raise cAMP, and amplify glucose-triggered calcium-dependent insulin-granule release; GLP-1 also reduces alpha-cell glucagon when glucose is elevated. The effect fades as glucose falls, explaining low intrinsic hypoglycemia risk. Sitagliptin does not directly close beta-cell KATP channels and has little direct gastric-emptying effect at DPP-4 exposures. Most drug is excreted unchanged through renal filtration and active secretion, so accumulation occurs as eGFR falls.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Postmarketing acute pancreatitis, including hemorrhagic or necrotizing cases, has occurred. Stop and evaluate persistent severe abdominal pain, especially if radiating to the back. Heart-failure risk was seen with other DPP-4 inhibitors; assess dyspnea, edema, and rapid weight gain in susceptible patients. Severe arthralgia and bullous pemphigoid can require discontinuation."),
      adverseEffects: ["Nasopharyngitis, headache, and mild GI symptoms", "Pancreatitis, severe hypersensitivity including angioedema/SJS, severe joint pain, and bullous pemphigoid", "Hypoglycemia mainly when combined with insulin or a sulfonylurea; renal accumulation if dose is not adjusted"],
