      description: "Linagliptin is an oral DPP-4 inhibitor that prolongs endogenous GLP-1 and GIP action, producing glucose-dependent insulin release and lower glucagon. It is weight-neutral and has low hypoglycemia risk when used alone. Its standout pharmacokinetic feature is minimal renal clearance: the usual dose is retained across kidney-function levels, which can simplify therapy in CKD. That convenience does not make it risk-free; pancreatitis, serious hypersensitivity, severe joint pain, bullous pemphigoid, and heart-failure symptoms still require recognition.",
      mechanism: "Linagliptin binds tightly and reversibly to the DPP-4 enzyme, preventing cleavage of active GLP-1 and GIP. The preserved hormones activate beta-cell Gs-coupled receptors, increase cAMP, and amplify insulin exocytosis only when glucose metabolism is already signaling a need. GLP-1-mediated alpha-cell effects reduce glucagon and hepatic glucose output. Linagliptin does not directly activate the GLP-1 receptor or close KATP channels, explaining modest weight effect and low intrinsic hypoglycemia. It is a P-glycoprotein substrate and weak-to-moderate CYP3A4 substrate but is eliminated predominantly through bile/gut, so kidney failure has limited effect on exposure.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Acute pancreatitis, serious hypersensitivity, severe disabling arthralgia, and bullous pemphigoid have occurred with DPP-4 inhibitors. Heart-failure risk was observed with other members of the class; in patients with prior heart failure or renal impairment, monitor dyspnea, edema, and rapid weight gain and reassess the benefit-risk balance."),
