      description: "Semaglutide is a long-acting GLP-1 receptor agonist that amplifies glucose-dependent insulin release, suppresses inappropriate glucagon, slows early gastric emptying, and increases satiety. The combined pancreas-gut-brain effect lowers A1c and body weight without independently forcing insulin during low glucose. Nausea is mechanistic, not proof of toxicity by itself, but persistent vomiting can cause dehydration and kidney injury. Rapid large glucose improvement can temporarily worsen existing diabetic retinopathy, and delayed gastric emptying matters before anesthesia because retained stomach contents can be aspirated.",
      mechanism: "Semaglutide is a modified analog of GLP-1, an intestinal incretin hormone, engineered to resist DPP-4 degradation and bind albumin for prolonged exposure. It activates the Gs-coupled GLP-1 receptor on beta cells, increasing adenylate cyclase, cAMP, PKA/Epac signaling, calcium-dependent insulin exocytosis, and insulin synthesis when glucose is elevated. It lowers alpha-cell glucagon during hyperglycemia, reduces hepatic glucose output, slows gastric delivery most prominently early in treatment, and activates hypothalamic/hindbrain satiety circuits. Low intrinsic hypoglycemia follows from glucose-dependent beta-cell amplification. The long half-life explains weekly injection, slow titration, prolonged adverse effects, and the recommendation to stop well before planned pregnancy.",
