      usedToTreat: "Severe hypoglycemia when a patient cannot safely take oral carbohydrate; selected injectable products are also diagnostic GI-motility inhibitors. Ready-to-use injection, nasal powder, autoinjector, and reconstitution kits differ. Emergency assistance and follow-up carbohydrate remain necessary.",
      description: "Glucagon is a 29-amino-acid counterregulatory hormone that tells the liver to release stored glucose. It is lifesaving when severe hypoglycemia prevents swallowing and IV access is not immediately available. Glucagon does not contain glucose: it spends hepatic glycogen, so response can be poor after prolonged fasting, severe malnutrition, adrenal insufficiency, heavy alcohol use, or advanced liver disease. It commonly causes vomiting; place an unconscious patient on the side when feasible and give oral carbohydrate only after awakening and safe swallowing.",
      mechanism: "Glucagon binds the hepatic Gs-coupled glucagon receptor, activates adenylate cyclase, raises cAMP, and activates PKA. PKA activates phosphorylase kinase and glycogen phosphorylase while inhibiting glycogen synthase, rapidly converting liver glycogen to glucose-1-phosphate and releasing glucose through hepatic glucose-6-phosphatase. It also supports gluconeogenesis. Skeletal muscle glycogen cannot directly correct blood glucose because muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase. Extrahepatic glucagon relaxes GI smooth muscle. In pheochromocytoma, glucagon can trigger massive catecholamine release; in insulinoma, the glucose rise can provoke secondary insulin release and recurrent hypoglycemia.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Glucagon may fail when hepatic glycogen is depleted; if the patient does not respond, give IV glucose as soon as access is available and evaluate the cause. It is contraindicated in pheochromocytoma and insulinoma. Vomiting creates aspiration risk, and severe hypoglycemia can recur after the brief rescue if long-acting insulin or a secretagogue remains active."),
      adverseEffects: ["Nausea, vomiting, headache, dizziness, tachycardia, and transient blood-pressure change", "Hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis and rare necrolytic migratory erythema with repeated exposure", "Recurrent hypoglycemia after glycogen is spent or causative drug action persists"],
      contraindications: ["Pheochromocytoma because catecholamine release can cause hypertensive crisis", "Insulinoma because secondary insulin release can cause recurrent hypoglycemia", "Product-specific hypersensitivity; do not force oral food into an unconscious patient"],
      nursingEssentials: ["Call for emergency help, administer the exact product correctly, position on the side when possible, monitor airway/glucose, and repeat only according to product instructions while arranging IV dextrose.", "Once awake and able to swallow, give rapid then longer-acting carbohydrate to restore glycogen and prevent recurrence; identify the insulin/secretagogue, meal, renal, alcohol, adrenal, or liver cause."],
      interactions: ["Beta blockers may produce a transient marked pulse/BP increase after glucagon.", "Indomethacin can blunt the glucose rise or paradoxically worsen hypoglycemia; warfarin effect may increase.", "Long-acting insulin and sulfonylureas can outlast rescue and require prolonged monitoring."],
      keyLabs: ["Immediate and serial glucose", "Potassium, renal/hepatic function, cortisol, and toxicology/cause-directed studies", "Airway, mental status, recurrence timing, and hepatic glycogen-depletion clues"],
      nclexTraps: ["Glucagon mobilizes stored glucose; it does not supply glucose.", "Turn the patient to the side because vomiting is common.", "After recovery, carbohydrate and cause correction are still required; one glucagon dose is not definitive treatment for a long-acting overdose."],
      populationRisks: populationRisks("Weight-based dosing applies to some products/younger children; every caregiver should practice the exact device before an emergency.", "Malnutrition, liver disease, alcohol use, frailty, and beta-blockers can change efficacy or hemodynamic response.", "Glucagon does not appear to cross the placenta substantially and is used for emergencies when needed; lactation exposure is expected to be low because it is a peptide, but clinical guidance remains product specific."),
      sourceNote: "Current U.S. glucagon labeling: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=3d8c92cf-9263-4126-8a5b-4cd761e31bc1",
