      usedToTreat: "Adjunct to nutrition and activity for type 2 diabetes, especially postmeal glucose excursions. It is taken with the first bite of each main meal because its target is carbohydrate digestion in the intestinal lumen. It does not treat type 1 diabetes or DKA.",
      description: "Acarbose is a minimally absorbed carbohydrate-digestion inhibitor that delays the postmeal glucose rise. It blocks intestinal brush-border alpha-glucosidases and pancreatic alpha-amylase, so complex starches and sucrose are broken into absorbable monosaccharides more slowly. Undigested carbohydrate reaches colonic bacteria, which explains flatulence, abdominal discomfort, and diarrhea. Used alone it does not force insulin and rarely causes hypoglycemia; if insulin or a secretagogue causes a low, use dextrose rather than sucrose because acarbose is blocking sucrose cleavage.",
      mechanism: "Acarbose is a competitive digestive-enzyme inhibitor. It inhibits the pancreatic alpha-amylase enzyme, which cuts starch into oligosaccharides, and membrane-bound intestinal alpha-glucosidase enzymes, which create absorbable glucose from oligosaccharides and disaccharides. Delayed hydrolysis spreads carbohydrate absorption across a longer interval and lowers the early postprandial peak without causing malabsorption of pure glucose. Bacterial fermentation of carbohydrate delivered to the colon generates hydrogen, methane, short-chain fatty acids, gas, and osmotic water. The mechanism explains first-bite timing and why oral D-glucose/dextrose works for rescue while table sugar, sucrose, is too slow.",
