      aliases: ["Xenical", "Alli", "orlistate", "lipase inhibitor weight loss", "oily stool weight medicine", "fat blocker"],
      brandExamples: ["Xenical", "Alli"],
      class: "Locally acting gastric and pancreatic lipase inhibitor for weight management",
      classPathway: ["Weight-management medication", "GI lipase inhibitor", "Dietary triglyceride malabsorption", "Peripheral nonstimulant obesity pharmacotherapy"],
      usedToTreat: "Long-term weight management with a reduced-calorie diet in adults meeting product-specific BMI criteria, including weight-loss maintenance after prior loss. Prescription Xenical and lower-dose OTC Alli differ. It acts only on dietary fat present in a meal, so the dose is omitted when the meal is missed or contains no fat.",
      description: "Orlistat is a nonstimulant weight-management drug that stays mainly in the GI tract and blocks digestion of dietary triglyceride. About one-quarter to one-third of meal fat at prescription dosing is not absorbed and leaves in stool. That directly explains oily spotting, fecal urgency, flatus with discharge, and leakage, especially after a high-fat meal. It also reduces absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, K and beta-carotene, so a separated daily multivitamin is part of therapy rather than optional decoration.",
      mechanism: "Orlistat is a mechanism-based lipase-enzyme inhibitor. It inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipase by forming a covalent bond with each enzyme's active-site serine in the stomach and small-intestinal lumen. Inactivated lipase cannot hydrolyze dietary triglycerides into absorbable free fatty acids and monoglycerides, so intact fat passes distally and is excreted. Minimal systemic absorption explains the absence of central stimulant effects. Unabsorbed fat produces predictable steatorrhea, while reduced fat and bile-acid handling lowers fat-soluble vitamin and some medication absorption. Increased unbound intestinal oxalate can raise urinary oxalate, contributing to calcium-oxalate stones or oxalate nephropathy in susceptible patients.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Rare severe hepatocellular injury and acute liver failure, oxalate nephropathy/kidney stones, cholelithiasis, and major drug malabsorption can occur. Stop and evaluate jaundice, dark urine, pruritus, severe abdominal pain, or worsening renal function. Separate and monitor cyclosporine, levothyroxine, warfarin, antiepileptics, antiretrovirals, amiodarone, and fat-soluble vitamins according to labeling."),
      adverseEffects: ["Oily spotting, fatty/oily stool, flatus with discharge, fecal urgency, increased defecation, and incontinence", "Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, cholelithiasis, and rare severe liver injury", "Hyperoxaluria, calcium-oxalate nephrolithiasis/oxalate nephropathy, pancreatitis, and medication malabsorption"],
      contraindications: ["Pregnancy", "Chronic malabsorption syndrome or cholestasis", "Use caution with prior hyperoxaluria/stones, CKD, eating disorder, significant liver disease, or essential interacting therapy"],
