      usedToTreat: "Chronic weight management with reduced-calorie nutrition and increased activity in adults with obesity or overweight plus a qualifying weight-related condition. It is not approved to treat depression, smoking cessation, or opioid-use disorder in this combination. Response is assessed after 12 weeks at maintenance dose; if at least 5 percent of baseline weight has not been lost, labeling directs discontinuation because meaningful sustained benefit is unlikely.",
      description: "Naltrexone/bupropion ER combines two CNS mechanisms to reduce hunger and food reward. Bupropion raises norepinephrine and dopamine signaling and activates hypothalamic POMC neurons; those neurons release alpha-MSH to signal satiety but also beta-endorphin, which feeds back through mu-opioid receptors and turns them down. Naltrexone blocks that opioid brake, prolonging POMC output, while both drugs also influence reward circuits. The combination can raise blood pressure, cause seizures, alter mood, and block opioid analgesia; it is not a benign appetite pill.",
      mechanism: "Bupropion inhibits norepinephrine and dopamine transporters and increases catecholamine signaling in hypothalamic and mesolimbic networks. POMC-neuron activation releases alpha-MSH, which stimulates melanocortin-4 receptors in satiety pathways, and beta-endorphin, which activates inhibitory mu-opioid autoreceptors. Naltrexone competitively antagonizes mu-opioid receptors, reducing beta-endorphin feedback and sustaining POMC firing. Naltrexone also reduces opioid-mediated reward reinforcement. Bupropion lowers seizure threshold and increases sympathetic tone; naltrexone displaces opioids and prevents expected analgesia. Extended-release delivery and slow titration limit peaks, which is why tablets must be swallowed whole and not taken with a high-fat meal that raises exposure.",
      boxedWarning: "BOXED WARNING: Because the product contains bupropion, monitor for suicidal thoughts and behaviors, clinical worsening, agitation, and abrupt behavioral change, especially in children, adolescents, and young adults; Contrave is not approved for pediatric patients or for treatment of depression. Additional major risks are dose-related seizure, uncontrolled hypertension, precipitated opioid withdrawal, inability to obtain usual opioid analgesia, mania/psychosis, angle-closure glaucoma, and hepatotoxicity.",
      adverseEffects: ["Nausea, constipation, headache, vomiting, dizziness, insomnia, dry mouth, and diarrhea", "Increased blood pressure/heart rate, seizure, anxiety/agitation, mania/psychosis, and suicidal thinking", "Opioid withdrawal or blocked analgesia, liver injury, angle-closure glaucoma, and hypoglycemia as diabetes therapy becomes excessive during weight loss"],
      contraindications: ["Uncontrolled hypertension, seizure disorder, anorexia nervosa or bulimia, abrupt alcohol/benzodiazepine/barbiturate/antiepileptic withdrawal, or another bupropion-containing product", "Chronic opioid or opioid-agonist/partial-agonist use, acute opioid withdrawal, or insufficient opioid-free interval; MAOI use within 14 days", "Pregnancy and serious product hypersensitivity"],
      nursingEssentials: ["Confirm all opioid exposure, including tramadol, cough/diarrhea products, methadone, and buprenorphine, before starting. A positive opioid history is not a minor interaction because naltrexone can precipitate withdrawal and block emergency analgesia.", "Measure BP/pulse, mood, sleep, seizure risk, and percent weight response; discontinue if the label's 5-percent response threshold is not met after 12 weeks at maintenance dose."],
      interactions: ["Opioids are blocked; attempting to override blockade can cause fatal overdose when naltrexone wanes, and acute pain needs a planned nonopioid/anesthesia strategy.", "MAOIs are prohibited; CYP2B6 inhibitors raise bupropion, inducers lower it, and bupropion inhibits CYP2D6 drugs.", "High-fat meals raise exposure and seizure risk; dopaminergic drugs and other seizure-threshold-lowering agents add CNS toxicity."],
      keyLabs: ["Weight/BMI and percent change at the labeled checkpoint", "Blood pressure, pulse, glucose/A1c, renal/hepatic function", "Mood/suicidality, seizure/eating-disorder history, opioid-free status, and medication interaction review"],
      nclexTraps: ["Contrave contains bupropion but is not approved as an antidepressant or smoking-cessation regimen.", "Naltrexone does not merely make opioids weaker; it can precipitate withdrawal and block analgesia.", "The 5-percent checkpoint avoids ineffective chronic exposure; it is not an arbitrary toxicity cliff."],
      populationRisks: populationRisks("Not approved for pediatric patients; antidepressant suicidality warning language is particularly important in younger people.", "Hypertension, seizure vulnerability, cognitive effects, polypharmacy, renal/hepatic impairment, and need for opioid analgesia often outweigh benefit.", "Contraindicated in pregnancy because weight loss offers no benefit; bupropion and metabolites enter milk and naltrexone exposure requires individualized lactation assessment."),
