      combinationProduct: true,
      studentFacingCombinationCard: true,
      usedToTreat: "Long-term weight reduction and maintenance with reduced-calorie nutrition and activity in adults and label-specified adolescents with obesity, and adults with overweight plus a qualifying comorbidity. Unlike phentermine monotherapy's 'few weeks' label, Qsymia is chronic therapy with dose-specific response checkpoints and a pregnancy-prevention REMS.",
      description: "Phentermine/topiramate ER is a chronic weight-management combination of a norepinephrine-promoting sympathomimetic anorectic and topiramate, an anticonvulsant with GABA-A enhancement, AMPA/kainate glutamate-receptor inhibition, and carbonic-anhydrase inhibition; these converging pathways reduce appetite and food reward, although topiramate's exact weight-loss mechanism is not fully defined. It is not the same treatment as short-term phentermine alone. Adults are evaluated after 12 weeks at 7.5/46 mg: less than 3 percent loss leads to escalation or discontinuation. After 12 weeks at 15/92 mg, less than 5 percent loss leads to gradual discontinuation because meaningful continued benefit is unlikely. The highest dose must be tapered to avoid precipitating seizure. Topiramate exposure in early pregnancy can cause oral clefts and small-for-gestational-age birth, so pregnancy testing and effective contraception are central, not optional.",
      mechanism: "Phentermine increases central norepinephrine-dominant sympathomimetic signaling, reducing hunger while raising arousal, pulse, and blood pressure. Topiramate's weight mechanism is not fully defined; relevant actions include enhancement of GABA-A signaling, inhibition of AMPA/kainate glutamate receptors, voltage-gated sodium-channel effects, and carbonic-anhydrase inhibition, which can alter appetite, taste, reward, and energy intake. Carbonic-anhydrase inhibition lowers bicarbonate and raises urine pH, explaining metabolic acidosis, kidney stones, hypokalemia, and reduced sweating. Abrupt withdrawal removes anticonvulsant stabilization, explaining the required taper from 15/92 mg even in a patient without epilepsy.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Qsymia can cause fetal harm and is dispensed through a REMS with certified pharmacies. Obtain a negative pregnancy test before treatment and monthly during therapy; use effective contraception and stop immediately if pregnancy occurs. Also monitor suicidal thoughts, cognitive/mood change, glaucoma, myopia, metabolic acidosis, kidney stones, hypokalemia, reduced sweating/hyperthermia, renal decline, and increased heart rate."),
      adverseEffects: ["Paresthesia, dizziness, altered taste, insomnia, constipation, dry mouth, and increased heart rate", "Cognitive slowing, word-finding difficulty, anxiety/depression, suicidal thinking, and seizure with abrupt high-dose withdrawal", "Metabolic acidosis, hypokalemia, kidney stones, oligohidrosis/hyperthermia, acute myopia/angle-closure glaucoma, and fetal oral clefts/SGA"],
      contraindications: ["Pregnancy, glaucoma, hyperthyroidism, MAOI use within 14 days, or serious hypersensitivity/idiosyncrasy to sympathomimetics/components", "Use caution and dose limits with renal/hepatic impairment, nephrolithiasis, acidosis, mood/cognitive disorder, or high heart-rate risk", "Do not duplicate phentermine or topiramate and do not combine with other weight-loss products without evidence-based specialist review"],
      nursingEssentials: ["Track exact strength and response checkpoint: after 12 weeks at 7.5/46 mg, less than 3 percent adult weight loss triggers escalation/discontinuation; after 12 weeks at 15/92 mg, less than 5 percent triggers discontinuation.", "Taper 15/92 mg every other day for at least 1 week before stopping. Obtain monthly pregnancy tests and teach contraception, eye pain/vision emergency, cognition/mood, hydration, stones, and heat intolerance."],
